\chapter{Portable protocols and host services}
Portable language code should describe an operation without assuming a
particular operating-system integration. MuDSL's codec and I/O protocols make
that boundary explicit: the user supplies a backend, and the toolkit supplies
the composition and validation around it.

\section{Binary data}
\api{BinaryStream} buffers bytes and keeps a read cursor. Reads check the
remaining length before constructing a view. Integer operations specify
endianness instead of relying on the host representation. Regex-token reads
reject zero-length matches so repeated tokenization makes progress.

For serialization, decide whether a format is a durable file contract or an
internal transport. A durable format needs versioning, size limits, and a
definition of invalid input. A host-defined codec can make those choices
without changing the toolkit.

\section{MuLL native services}
MuLL exposes native services as ordinary record members containing callable
values. The host registers those records before scripts import packages.
The supplied standard library implements formatting, terminal output, math,
text processing, and list operations. An application can register additional
records for its own data model or file services using the same mechanism.

The runtime does not guess native signatures from a shared library. A C or C++
function can be wrapped in a C++ callable; a D function can be wrapped in a
delegate. The wrapper validates arguments and converts results into MuLL values.
